  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is logistic growth?",
      "a": "Growth that starts nearly exponential, then slows and levels off at a limit. Its graph is an S-shaped curve.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an S-curve in biology?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is carrying capacity?",
      "a": "The largest population the environment can support with its food, water and space. In the pond it is about 1000 fish.",
      "asks": [
        "What does carrying capacity mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "When is logistic growth fastest?",
      "a": "When the population is half the carrying capacity. Then there are many parents and still enough food.",
      "asks": [
        "Where is the steepest part of the S-curve?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the logistic equation?",
      "a": "dP by dt equals r P times 1 minus P over K, where r is the growth rate and K the carrying capacity. When P is small it acts like r P; near K the growth stops.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you write the logistic model?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How is logistic growth different from exponential growth?",
      "a": "Exponential growth speeds up forever. Logistic growth starts the same way, but slows as resources run out and levels off at the carrying capacity.",
      "asks": [
        "Logistic vs exponential?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The logistic equation is dP/dt = rP(1 − P/K), where K is the carrying capacity.",
    "Logistic growth is fastest when the population is K/2."
  ],
